au/Dallas Divide; Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.0131f538810dac521ad09e37656bcfc83b15cbff.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.0131f538810dac521ad09e37656bcfc83b15cbff.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued September 29 at 10:07PM MDT until October 1 at 6:00AM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of Colorado, including the following areas, Animas\nRiver Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, Northwest San Juan\nMountains, Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River, San Juan River\nBasin, Southwest San Juan Mountains and Uncompahgre Plateau and\nDallas Divide and southeast Utah, including the following areas,\nCanyonlands/Natural Bridges, La Sal and Abajo Mountains and\nSoutheast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...Through late Wednesday night.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding will be possible over burn scars, at low-water\ncrossings, and along creeks, streams, washes, arroyos and other\nflood-prone locations. Creeks and streams may rise out of their\nbanks. Unpaved roads could become muddy and impassable.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Periods of heavy rainfall, associated with remnant moisture from\nHurricane Polo, are expected to produce numerous showers and\nembedded thunderstorms. Heavy rain falling on saturated soils and\nburn scars could lead to localized flash flooding and rises on\nlocal rivers. Rainfall is expected to end by late Wednesday, but\nrivers may rise out of their banks overnight Wednesday even after\nrainfall has ended.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-01T06:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; San Juan River Basin","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.0131f538810dac521ad09e37656bcfc83b15cbff.002.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.0131f538810dac521ad09e37656bcfc83b15cbff.002.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-09-30T05:27:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 300527 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 1127 PM MDT Tue Sep 29 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Scattered to widespread precipitation showers and storms continue through Wednesday evening. - Flooding concerns are increased for rain-soaked terrain, especially within and adjacent to the southern San Juans. - A Flood Watch is in effect for southeast Ut","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-09-29T19:57:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION.. Rain and thunderstorms are expected to continue through Wednesday evening, primarily south of I-70. Prolonged or heavy periods of rainfall could lead to flooding, especially in areas that have recently received substantial rain. By Thursday high pressure begins to move into the area, leading to drier and warmer conditions. However, enough moisture remains to keep critical fire weathe","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":{"station_id":"oth01","station_name":"Olathe","distance_km":3.7,"soil_temp_5cm_f":61.59,"soil_temp_15cm_f":65.17,"soil_moisture_5cm":null,"soil_moisture_15cm":null,"precip_in":null,"eto_in":null,"vapor_pressure":null,"solar_radiation":0,"wind_speed_mph":1.14,"air_temp_f":55.76,"relative_humidity":0.891,"url":"https://coagmet.colostate.edu/station/oth01"},"aviation":{"icao":"KMTJ","distance_km":0,"raw_metar":"METAR KMTJ 300553Z AUTO 16006KT 10SM BKN110 14/11 A2992 RMK AO2 SLP069 T01390111 10183 20133 58005","temp_f":57,"wind_dir":160,"wind_kt":6,"gust_kt":null,"visibility":"10+","cover":"BKN","flight_category":"VFR","altimeter":1013.3,"observed":1790747580,"raw_taf":"TAF KMTJ 300522Z 3006/0106 12006KT P6SM FEW